WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.100
This episode was sponsored by Blinkist.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:08.340
Blinkist puts all of the need to know information
from thousands of nonfiction books and condenses

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:14.949
them down into just 15 minutes and you can
go to blinkist.com/microcosmos to learn more.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:30.089
Recently, we did our first ever Journey to
the Microcosmos livestream.

00:00:30.089 --> 00:00:34.190
Some of you may even have been there, whether
that’s because you wanted to see the microbes

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.010
that James, our master of microscopes, was
showing live from his own microscope, or because

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.969
you wanted to see our faces.

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:45.719
To be honest, we’d understand if you were
there mostly for the microbes, but whatever

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:48.449
the reason, thank you for joining us.

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:55.199
The real highlight though for all of us was
a moment that combined the best of microbes

00:00:55.199 --> 00:00:56.199
and faces.

00:00:56.199 --> 00:01:02.629
It is this moment right here, that look on
James’ face when he realized that something

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:05.290
very exciting had just appeared on the screen.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:10.122
Here, I’ll just play it and you can experience
the excitement again in real time.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.020
The thing that had James so excited was that
green, glow stick-looking ciliate called Spirostomum

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:49.420
semivirescens, it’s a large ciliate he had
been working to find for over three years,

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:55.140
and it just happened to show up during our
livestream.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.930
His mentor Professor Genoveva Esteban was
watching the livestream from the UK, where

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.509
she works at Bournemouth University.

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:07.180
And when the moment of recognition hit both
her and James, she texted “omg” followed

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.550
by approximately 14 exclamation points.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:17.910
Look, 2021 has been a strange year, and the
pandemic has led to lots of changes in how

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.410
we share and talk about things.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:25.030
But there’s some joy to be had in the fact
that we could share this moment as a community

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:31.750
spread all around the world…all of us peering
through a microscope as James guided us through

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:35.080
what we were seeing.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.540
But of course, the real question is why were
James and his professor so excited?

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:49.050
Well, there are currently eight known spirostomums
in the world, their bodies all long and contractile

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.050
and twisty.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:55.160
Some of these specimens can grow as much as
4 mm in length, making them both visible to

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.570
the naked eye and potentially easy to confuse
with worms.

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:04.900
And while some of these species are abundant,
Spirostomum semivirescens has been a much

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.200
rarer find.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.271
It’s surprising, when you look at it.

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:15.340
After all, this is a 2 mm long, bright green
ciliate slithering through a field of smaller

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.120
organisms and debris.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.140
It’s just so glaringly there.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.380
How could it be difficult to find?

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.450
Well, according to the reported observations,
pretty difficult.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.850
There’s a record from Germany dating back
to 1852.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.720
And in 2009, Professor Esteban and her team
recorded their presence for the first time

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:38.860
in the UK.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:44.500
In 2015, they followed that up with a study
of Spirostomum semivirescens found in two

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:49.810
Swedish ponds: one in farmland, and the other
in an ancient forest.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:55.370
Now, it’s hard to pin down exactly why this
species might be so rare.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.840
Some microbes are just like that, probably
because they’re particular about what kind

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.290
of light or food they like to have around
them.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:08.450
The thing about being rare though is that
sure, it might take a while to find a spot

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.260
where the organism likes to live.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.060
But once you find their home, they can turn
out to be fairly abundant.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.370
We talk about ponds like they’re one big
habitat.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:24.189
But for microbes, they’re actually many,
many, many habitats stacked on top of and

00:04:24.189 --> 00:04:30.580
below and around each other, with fuzzy boundaries
defined by chemical gradients and temperature

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:36.229
changes and physical entities that create
many different types of homes that suit many

00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:41.400
different types of organisms.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.500
And so finding organisms like Spirostomum
semivirescens can sometimes be a matter of

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:52.580
sampling many different places, or it can
be a matter of sampling the same site over

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.250
and over again.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:58.639
Either way, microscopy rewards the patient
and diligent.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:04.030
And the rarity of Spirostomum semivirescens
is made all the more interesting because of

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:05.648
its size.

00:05:18.156 --> 00:05:22.870
There’s this idea in microbiology, maybe
you’ve even heard of it: “everything is

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:27.590
everywhere, but the environment selects.”

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:31.389
That phrase was coined by the Dutch microbiologist
Lourens G.M.

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:34.159
Baas Becking in 1934.

00:05:34.159 --> 00:05:38.470
Becking himself was drawing on the work of
another Dutch microbiologist named Martinus

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:44.280
Beijerinck, who had observed that using different
culture conditions allowed him to isolate

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.500
different types of bacteria from his samples.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:53.259
When extended beyond the confines of a lab
culture, the premise is this: microbes are

00:05:53.259 --> 00:05:54.259
tiny.

00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:55.979
Like super small, right?

00:05:55.979 --> 00:06:01.700
But they’re also able to form very large
populations, growing massive in number, if

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.840
small in size.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.250
And as a result of their small size and large
populations—not to mention their ability

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:14.800
to form protective cysts— it is not hard
for microbes to disperse and spread.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.490
There are many of them.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.460
They can stick to the legs of ducks and turtles.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:21.360
They can blow on the wind.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.460
We find the same species of microbes all around
the world.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:29.009
They find it easy to move around.

00:06:29.009 --> 00:06:32.210
But of course, not everything is actually
everywhere.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:36.449
How could they be, when there are so many
different types of places to be.

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:43.189
Well, that’s where the second half of the
hypothesis comes in: “but the environment selects.”

00:06:43.189 --> 00:06:49.249
The microbes may disperse easily and spread
widely, but what survives is the result of

00:06:49.249 --> 00:06:52.680
the many environmental factors they land in.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.430
But of course there are limits to this.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.240
Microbes find it easy to move around, kangaroos
do not.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:06.129
There’s a size at which it gets more difficult
to be everywhere

00:07:06.129 --> 00:07:11.509
Spirostomum semivirescens’s large size makes
it an exciting organism to consider against

00:07:11.509 --> 00:07:16.809
this idea of everything being everywhere because
it’s like pushing the limit of what makes

00:07:16.809 --> 00:07:19.169
a microbe easy to disperse.

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:26.340
And that makes the discovery of the species
at distant ponds all the more compelling.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:31.289
But while “Everything is everywhere, but
the environment selects” may just be a single

00:07:31.289 --> 00:07:38.800
sentence, the debate it has inspired has spanned
many decades worth of research papers.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.809
There are other scientists who have reported
bacteria that appear to be rendered distinct

00:07:43.809 --> 00:07:49.610
by their location, making their biology a
function not just of environment, but of whatever

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:53.560
history separated them from other similar
species.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:59.490
The nuances of how to think about and interpret
“everything is everywhere, but the environment

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:08.559
selects'' is itself a reflection of just how
remarkable microbes are, and also the dialogue

00:08:08.559 --> 00:08:12.629
that shapes science over long periods of time.

00:08:12.629 --> 00:08:18.069
And while our Spirostomum semivirescens cannot
bring the conversation to a close, we are

00:08:18.069 --> 00:08:24.685
excited to have been able to interrupt it
for a moment, with just a little bit of new information.

00:08:29.319 --> 00:08:34.150
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us, and also to those of you who were there

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:40.490
for this moment on our journey as we explore
the unseen world that surrounds us.

